The `as TestStatusValue` cast bypasses TypeScript without a runtime guard. `statusValueToStoryIdsMap` is only initialized with the five test-status keys (`pending`, `success`, `error`, `warning`, `unknown`). If `status.value` is ever `'status-value:new'`, `'status-value:modified'`, or `'status-value:affected'` under the vitest type ID — for example if a future change causes the vitest addon to emit one of these values, or if a change-detection addon shares the same type ID — `statusValueToStoryIdsMap[status.value as TestStatusValue]` resolves to `undefined` and `.push()` throws `TypeError: Cannot read properties of undefined`. The cast tells TypeScript to trust the narrowing but provides no actual runtime guarantee. Add an explicit guard:
```typescript
if (status.value in statusValueToStoryIdsMap) {
  statusValueToStoryIdsMap[status.value as TestStatusValue].push(status.storyId);
}
```
This keeps the map correct and makes the implicit assumption explicit.
